{"t":1792344144,"office":"LWX","station":"KDCA","point":"38.8894,-77.0352","field":"38.9429,-76.5684","field_station":"KNAK","afd":{"id":"8f8acae6-a67e-4438-99f3-1c03cd0ca521","office":"LWX","issuanceTime":"2026-10-18T14:13:00+00:00","productText":"\n000\nFXUS61 KLWX 181413\nAFDLWX\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Baltimore MD/Washington DC\n1013 AM EDT Sun Oct 18 2026\n\n.WHAT HAS CHANGED...\nThe growing season has ended west of the Blue Ridge Mountains.\nThe need for Frost Advisories tonight in the piedmont will be \ndetermined by early afternoon.\n\n&&\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n- 1) Below normal temperatures with frost and freeze\n  concerns continue through Monday morning.\n \n- 2) Potential for isolated showers and breezy conditions next\n  week as a Nor'easter lurks offshore.\n\n&&\n\n.DISCUSSION...\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Below normal temperatures with frost and freeze\nconcerns continue through Monday morning.\n\nSurface high pressure settles over the Mid-Atlantic today\nthrough Monday with below normal temperatures expected. High \ntemperatures today rise into the 50s to low 60s across the \nforecast area with overnight lows tonight dropping into the 30s \nto mid 40s. Additional Frost Advisories will likely be needed \nfor Monday morning where the growing season continues east of\nthe Blue Ridge. Some pockets could fall below freezing, but will\nneed to evaluate incoming guidance to determine if this will\noccur on a widespread enough basis to need a Freeze Warning.\n\nTemperatures will climb slightly Monday and Monday night, with \nadditional Frost Advisories appearing unlikely.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Potential for isolated showers and breezy \nconditions next week as a Nor'easter lurks offshore.\n\nParticularly for locations along or east of I-95, a developing \nNor'easter off of the Mid-Atlantic coast guides weather conditions \nlate next week. East of I-95, scattered shower potential, wind \nstrength, and coastal flood risk are dependent on the track of this \nsystem. Regardless of where this Nor-easter tracks, locations \nfurther inland, particularly west of the Blue Ridge and Catoctin \nmountain ranges, are expected to remain dry in part due to \ndownsloping west of these mountain ranges. Daily daytime \ntemperatures are expected to remain fairly consistent throughout \nthis time period reaching into mid to upper 60s (50s at higher \nelevations). Cooler than average overnight temperatures on Monday \npersist through the end of the week.\n\nAn omega block setup and a predominantly zonal flow next week \ngenerally leads to uncertain but weaker systems. Current guidance \ncontinues to exhibit moderate spread when it comes to the placement \nof the low and timing as it meanders along the coast. On Monday, a \ndeveloping low pressure system is located off the North Carolina \ncoast. During the middle of next week, this Nor'easter crawls \nnorthward, remaining off the Mid-Atlantic coast. Guidance differs \nmost at towards the end of the week and into the weekend. Most \nsolutions keep this Nor'easter well off shore, maintaining dry \nconditions throughout our region. Some guidance meanders the low \ntoward the coast Thursday and Friday, increasing the potential for \nisolated showers and breezier conditions exclusively along and east \nof the I-95 corridor. As for any marine and coastal flooding \nconcerns, see the corresponding discussions below.\n\n&&\n\n.AVIATION /14Z SUNDAY THROUGH THURSDAY/...\nNorth/northeasterly winds are expected today and Monday, \ngusting around 15 knots each afternoon. Winds shift to\nnorthwesterly on Tuesday, gusting 15 to 20 knots in the\nafternoon. VFR conditions are expected each day with surface\nhigh pressure yielding dry conditions. \n\nTuesday through Saturday, primarily VFR conditions are expected \namongst all terminals with periods of sub-VFR mainly to the east, \npotentially including BWI, DCA, and MTN. A few showers are possible \non the east side of the ZDC airspace with the Nor'easter lurking \noffshore. Winds will generally be out of the northeast at 10 to 20 \nkts with gusts up to 30 kts, especially near the waters.\n\n&&\n\n.MARINE...\nSmall Craft Advisory conditions continue today, though gradually\ncontracting toward southern Maryland, with northwest winds \ngusting around 20 knots. Elevated winds continue through early \nnext week as a Nor'easter develops off the Carolina coast with \nSCA criteria winds possible. \n\nThrough Thursday, a developing Nor'easter off the Mid- Atlantic\ncoast delivers a prolonged period of SCA conditions. Over the \nwider, central Chesapeake Bay, gale condtions are a possibility \nthis period. Confidence is currently low for gales based upon \nthe track of the system with projected winds out of the \nnortheast at 10- 20 kts with gusts up to 30 kts at times.\n\n&&\n\n.TIDES/COASTAL FLOODING...\nTides are snapping back today as northerly winds slacken. A \ndeveloping Nor'easter off the Mid-Atlantic coast will lead to \ntidal anomalies continuing to rise into the upcoming week. \nSensitive locations (i.e. Annapolis) will have a chance to reach\nminor flooding thresholds during the high tide cycle tonight, \nand again during the middle of next week. Considerable \nuncertainty remains on how exactly this Nor'easter will track \nand subsequently affect the tide levels.\n\n&&\n\n.LWX WATCHES/WARNINGS/ADVISORIES...\nDC...None.\nMD...Small Craft Advisory until 11 AM EDT this morning for MDZ008.\nVA...None.\nWV...None.\nMARINE...Small Craft Advisory until 11 AM EDT this morning for ANZ530-\n     531-538-539.\n     Small Craft Advisory until 8 AM EDT Monday for ANZ532>534-537-\n     540>543.\n\n&&\n\n$$\n\nDISCUSSION...ADS/AVS/KVH\nAVIATION...ADS/AVS/KVH\nMARINE...ADS/AVS/KVH\n"},"forecast":{"t":1792344100,"days":{"2026-10-18":{"hi":61,"lo":42,"pop":0,"short":"Sunny"},"2026-10-19":{"hi":64,"lo":48,"pop":0,"short":"Sunny"},"2026-10-20":{"hi":68,"lo":48,"pop":1,"short":"Sunny"},"2026-10-21":{"hi":64,"lo":48,"